[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue and marked known. Its own cause table says
otherwise: the reports in it come from more than one problem, and one of those problems has no
ticket. This page splits #4471 by what each report describes, with one ticket per problem.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket, its own fix and its own count.
</Callout>

## The problems in #4471

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  tickets={{
    parser: 'https://github.com/VasilyShelkov/support-kb/issues/1',
    hint: 'https://github.com/VasilyShelkov/support-kb/issues/2',
    'one-day': 'https://github.com/VasilyShelkov/support-kb/issues/3',
  }}
/>

## What each problem is

**`parser`** ([ticket](https://github.com/VasilyShelkov/support-kb/issues/1)). The shared parser
reads every date month first. A tenant that writes the day first books 7 April and gets 4 July.
Every tenant on file writes the day first, so the reports come from all of them: through the app,
by email and through the booking-v2 form.

**`hint`** ([ticket](https://github.com/VasilyShelkov/support-kb/issues/2)). The booking-v3 form
prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. The tenant's people type the month first, as the hint
tells them, and the form reads the day first. The symptom is the same swap, but it is a different
bug: a fix to the parser leaves the hint wrong. Only tenants on booking-v3 have it, and only for
bookings made through that form.

**`one-day`** ([ticket](https://github.com/VasilyShelkov/support-kb/issues/3)). Dates that move
by one day: a Tuesday shown as the Monday before, an invite that says one day while the calendar
says the day before, a client in Toronto who gets a different day than a client in Lisbon for the
same meeting. A date one day off is not a swapped month. These are the reports the desk already
read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`). The desk files them under `parser` by tenant and
counts them there, so they count under both.

## How the reports are counted

The counts are the desk's own: the cause table on #4471, as exported.

- One report per id. The export pages overlap, so some reports come twice.
- The cause comes from the tenant: the locale it writes dates in and the booking form it runs.
  Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser, so it is not used.
- A report the desk read as a one-day move stays counted where the desk files it, under its
  tenant's cause, and is counted again under `one-day`.

## Lessons

- Read what a page shows, not only what it says. The #4471 page said one issue; its own table
  showed more than one.
- The subjects in #4471 all say "wrong day". The tenant, the way the report came in and the body
  say which problem it is.

